        ":question/id": "e2d5cf9e-961d-5b9b-b856-23fa976de096",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "Let $f(x,y)=x + 2 y,$ where $x=3 s + 2 t$ and $y=s + 3 t.$ Calculate $\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202s}.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202s}=\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}\\cdot \\frac{\u2202x}{\u2202s}+\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}\\cdot \\frac{\u2202y}{\u2202s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x} &=1,\\,\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202x}{\u2202s} & =3, \\\\ \\frac{\u2202y}{\u2202s} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202s} & =\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}\\cdot \\frac{\u2202x}{\u2202s}+\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}\\cdot \\frac{\u2202y}{\u2202s} \\\\  & =(1)\\cdot (3)+(2)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =(3)+(2) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
